.TH MODIFY_LDT 2 "March 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
modify_ldt \- read or update the current process local descriptor table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/ldt.h>
.PP
.BI "int modify_ldt(int " func ", void *" ptr ", unsigned long " bytecount ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR modify_ldt ()
system call reads or updates the Local Descriptor Table (LDT) associated
with the calling process.
.PP
The
.I func
argument selects the operation:
.TP
.B LDT_READ
Copy the current process LDT image to the buffer referenced by
.IR ptr .
At most
.I bytecount
bytes are copied, rounded only by the current LDT size.
The return value is the number of bytes copied.
.TP
.B LDT_WRITE
Interpret
.I ptr
as a pointer to a
.I struct user_desc
and write or clear one LDT entry.
The kernel currently accepts only one descriptor per call, so
.I bytecount
must equal
.BR sizeof(struct user_desc) .
Entries are validated as user descriptors and are installed with DPL 3.
.TP
.B LDT_READ_DEFAULT
Return the default-LDT image size for compatibility.
Substrate currently returns 0 and does not expose a separate default LDT
payload.
.PP
A descriptor with zero base, zero limit, and
.B seg_not_present
set clears the target entry.
.PP
The exported
.I struct user_desc
fields follow the Linux-compatible layout used by both
.BR modify_ldt (2)
and
.BR set_thread_area (2)
on i386.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR modify_ldt ()
returns the number of bytes copied for
.BR LDT_READ ,
0 for successful
.BR LDT_WRITE
and
.BR LDT_READ_DEFAULT ,
or \-1 on error with
.I errno
set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I ptr
is not accessible for the requested copyin or copyout operation.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I func
is unsupported, the descriptor payload is malformed, the entry number is out
of bounds, or
.I bytecount
does not match the required size for the requested operation.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate or grow per-process LDT storage.
.SH NOTES
The native Substrate path currently exposes only per-process user LDT entries.
It does not permit installing kernel descriptors, call gates, or other system
segment forms through this interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ldtctl (1),
.BR set_thread_area (2)
.SH COLOPHON
